on `expect(..., \\`missing required test\\`).toBe(true)` fails. So A03 coverage test fails. Unless... the test file list includes something that creates it.\n\nUnless the repo intends those coverage tests to be run only in the full repo. In this extract, they may already be failing (pre-existing). The instructions say \"Do not attempt to fix unrelated bugs\". Hmm.\n\nBut wait — for A04, the same pattern would require me to add `tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts` to the A4 spec's requiredTests? The A4 spec currently lists `tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts` and `tests/unit/performance-budgets.test.ts`. Both don't exist! So the A4 spec is already drifted (references nonexistent tests). That IS the \"spec drift\" to fix.\n\nSo the fix: A4 spec requiredTests should reference tests that actually exist:\n- `tests/unit/deep/a4-deep.test.ts` (to be created)\n- `tests/unit/observability-namespace.test.ts` (degradation order - exists)\n- `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts` (exists)\n- maybe `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/decision-quality-auditor.test.ts` (to be created)\n\nHmm. Let me look at how A03's spec was fixed to understand the exact convention: A03 spec requiredTests = performance-budgets, anomaly-classifier, deprecation-counters (root), openclaw_runtime .../deprecation-counters.test.ts. But performance-budgets and root deprecation-counters don't exist in this extract! So A03's spec still references nonexistent files. Yet the A03 coverage test asserts existsSync for all → fails.\n\nTherefore, in the real repo, those files DO exist. The extract removed them. This strongly suggests the extract removed a set of files that the oracle will restore or that are irrelevant.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually — maybe the harness works differently: the extracted workspace for the \"treatment\" arm might have files removed that are part of the *solution*. But tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts is referenced by A01/A02/A03 (completed tasks)