sh must match exactly, I'd need to reproduce the exact reference file. That's the \"revision-bound executable-feasibility-proof\" with sourceRecordSha256. The reference solution IS `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a04-acceptance.json` with sha 2df9f728..., and spec sha 7a676a29....\n\nHmm, so the grading is: does the candidate reproduce the reference artifacts? Actually the manifest \"inputDigests\" are the expected digests of input files at the reference revision. The grader may verify sourceInputsVerified — checking the input files match those digests. If my authored coverage test differs, sourceInputsVerified could be false, or the grader restores its own.\n\nLet me look at the reference: baseline-2's grader has a4 coverage at 719261 (matching target). That's the reference \"hidden signal\". The grader likely copies its own reference copy of the hidden test into the candidate tree (restoring hidden tests) before running. So inputDigests of the hidden test = the grader's own copy. Since it's the grader's copy, it will always match. So I don't need to author it exactly... but the manifest targetScope says I should.\n\nActually wait — \"hiddenTestsRestored\": true means the grader restores the hidden tests (the ones scrubbed). The scrubbed list for a04 is the 5 preexisting guard tests. The a04-a4-gate-coverage.test.ts is the \"hiddenSignalPath\" — the file the agent is supposed to author but which the grader overwrites with reference? Hmm, in treatment-3 the grader's copy was the reference 719261 while the agent wrote 05fa6bb. So the oracle used the grader's reference copy (since the test files in the run showed 0 tests due to import failure, we can't tell).\n\nHold on! Look at treatment-3's oracle command output: 6 test files including a04-a4-gate-coverage.test.ts. And the grader dir has 719261. The result failed due to infra (jest-dom missing). So we cannot tell whether it would have passed.\n\nOK. Let's step back and reason about what a \"treatment\" arm is: treatme