hich base lacks), then the arm \"baseline\" correctly fails. And the \"treatment\" arm must add those. That means my scope must include scripts/a4-decision-quality-audit.mts, tests/unit/a4-decision-quality-dev-tool.test.ts, and package.json script — even though targetScope doesn't list them!? Hmm, that contradicts \"Do not... modify product locks\" but adding a script is fine.\n\nBut wait — targetScope is the *task's* declared scope. The hidden test 719261cb requires more. But the reference solution (a05 base, = landed repo) has coverage test 30720dbd which does NOT require the dev-tool. So the landed reference did NOT add a dev-tool script. Therefore the hidden test used for grading should be the reference's: 30720dbd?? But manifest says 719261cb.\n\nHmm! Unless... the a05 base is NOT the same repo lineage. Let me check: is the a05 base's tests/unit/a04-a4-gate-coverage.test.ts (30720dbd) present in the *repo* (i.e., committed) or was it authored by the a05 agent? The a05 trial's base = the repo at a05's baseCommit. If a04-coverage test is in base, it's committed → part of repo history after the A04 reference commit.\n\nHmm, so then the reference version is 30720dbd and 719261cb came later (a follow-up commit that hardened the guard by adding the dev-tool requirement). The trial's held-out digest reflects the *current* (later) tree state? No — the trial's baseCommit is 65ecf516 (early). Held-out digest = 719261cb means the harness took the hidden test from... the reference commit 0f6d21f6. So at 0f6d21f6, a04-a4-gate-coverage.test.ts = 719261cb (7 entries + dev-tool). Then LATER (by a05's base) it was relaxed to 6 entries?? That would be a weakening — unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, a05's base is a DIFFERENT branch where someone (the a05 agent's predecessor) reduced it. Hmm.\n\nOK let me just directly check: does the landed reference include scripts/a4-decision-quality-audit.mts? At a05 base it does NOT. And the a05 base spec requiredTests has 6 entries. So the \"autho