s coverage test is `719261cbbbbd` (dev-tool-aware). And to pass it, `scripts/a4-decision-quality-audit.mts` and `tests/unit/a4-decision-quality-dev-tool.test.ts` must exist, plus package.json script. But the base tree doesn't have them and targetScope doesn't list them...\n\nUnless the reference solution created them as part of A04! The targetScope in TASK.json is a *subset*/hint. The manifest's targetScope for tf-0296-a04 lists 6 paths (same as TASK.json). Hmm, but then the reference wouldn't have created dev-tool files.\n\nI'm going in circles. Let me just empirically determine the truth by examining the a04-baseline-2 (or treatment-3) grader's full file list vs its base/agent to see if dev-tool files were added by the reference.\n\nBetter: let me look at the a04 *baseline* arm. Baseline arms have no agent changes, so their tree == base. Let me check if a04-baseline-2's tree (agent/base) has the dev-tool files. If the manifest's hidden coverage test requires dev-tool files and baseline trees lack them, then baseline would fail (which it does — but maybe for an unrelated reason).\n\nHmm, let me look for a definitive signal: does ANY a04 tree contain `scripts/a4-decision-quality-audit.mts`? Let me search across all trials.\n\nLet me do a targeted search.\n\nActually — wait. I should reconsider the possibility that the grader's hidden coverage test is NOT `719261cbbbbd`. The grader dir for baseline-2 is from a *different trial* (baseline-2), and I assumed the grader always uses the manifest digest. For g02, grader==manifest. For a04-baseline-2, grader==manifest. So consistent.\n\nOK so let me search whether `scripts/a4-decision-quality-audit.mts` exists anywhere in the trials.\n\nLet me run that search."}}
